thens the neural pathway connecting "tall shape/feeding time" to "food." The temporary note gets filed into the long-term filing cabinet. So, the goldfish doesn't need to "remember" you from three hours ago in its short-term memory. It simply retrieves the **long-term memory** it has already created: "That tall shape at this time of day in this spot equals food." The short-term memory was just the glue that allowed the initial association to be made and reinforced into a long-term memory. It's the same for us. You don't have to keep the memory of "red light means stop" in your short-term consciousness all day long. You learned it long ago, filed it away, and you just retrieve it when you see a red light. The goldfish is doing exactly the same thing when it sees you approach the tank.
